---
import DocsLayout from '../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';

// The registry CLI is published to npm as @dastaran/uni-kit. It copies a
// component's source and everything it imports into an application, so the
// files land in the reader's own tree to edit.
const command = 'npx @dastaran/uni-kit add slint sidebar';
---

<DocsLayout
  title="npm — GPUI Kit"
  description="The @dastaran/uni-kit package: install components into your own source tree."
>
  <h1>npm</h1>
  <p>
    The component registry ships as <code>@dastaran/uni-kit</code> on npm. It
    copies a component — and every file that component imports — into your
    project, where the source is yours to change.
  </p>

  <h2>Install a component</h2>
  <pre><code>{command}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Name one component or several: <code>add slint sidebar card button data-table</code>.
    <code>--dir</code> chooses where the files land; by default they go to
    <code>ui/components/</code>, and the command adds that directory to the Slint
    include path in your <code>build.rs</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>What it writes</h2>
  <p>
    Whole files, not a dependency. A screen pulls in its theme, its icons and the
    controls it composes, so an install is typically a few dozen files. Nothing is
    overwritten silently: the command reads every source before it writes the
    first one, and stops if any is missing.
  </p>

  <h2>The package</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@dastaran/uni-kit" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">npmjs.com/package/@dastaran/uni-kit</a>
      — the published package.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="https://github.com/MohsenDastaran/uni-kit/tree/main/registry" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">registry/</a>
      — the CLI source and the manifest of what each component installs.
    </li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
